00051  1     
00052  1     /* COMMAND           FUNCTION
00053  1        -------           --------
00054  1         A            APPEND LINES OF TEXT TO BUFFER
00055  1         B            MOVE TO BEGINNING OR END OF TEXT
00056  1         C            SKIP CHARACTERS
00057  1         D            DELETE CHARACTERS
00058  1         E            END OF EDIT
00059  1         F            FIND STRING IN CURRENT BUFFER
00060  1         H            MOVE TO TOP OF FILE (HEAD)
00061  1         I            INSERT   CHARACTERS FROM KEYBOARD
00062  1                      UP TO NEXT <ENDFILE>
00063  1         J            JUXTAPOSITION OPERATION - SEARCH FOR FIRST STRING,
00064  1                      INSERT SECOND STRING, DELETE UNTIL THIRD STRING
00065  1         K            DELETE LINES
00066  1         L            SKIP LINES
00067  1         M            MACRO DEFINITION (SEE COMMENT BELOW)
00068  1         N            FIND NEXT OCCURRENCE OF STRING
00069  1                      WITH AUTO SCAN THROUGH FILE
00070  1         O            RE-EDIT OLD FILE
00071  1         P            PAGE AND DISPLAY (MOVES UP OR DOWN 24 LINES AND
00072  1                      DISPLAYS 24 LINES)
00073  1         Q            QUIT EDIT WITHOUT UPDATING THE FILE
00074  1         R<FILENAME>  READ FROM FILE <FILENAME>.LIB UNTIL <ENDFILE> AND
00075  1                      INSERT INTO TEXT
00076  1         S            SEARCH FOR FIRST STRING, REPLACE BY SECOND STRING
00077  1         T            TYPE LINES
00078  1         U            TRANSLATE TO UPPER CASE (-U CHANGES TO NO TRANSLATE)
00079  1         W            WRITE LINES OF TEXT TO FILE
00080  1         X            TRANSFER (XFER) LINES TO TEMP FILE
00081  1         Z            SLEEP FOR 1/2 SECOND (USED IN MACROS TO STOP DISPLAY)
00082  1         <CR>         MOVE UP OR DOWN AND PRINT ONE LINE
00083  1     
00084  1    
00085  1        IN GENERAL, THE EDITOR ACCEPTS SINGLE LETTER COMMANDS WITH OPTIONAL
00086  1    INTEGER VALUES PRECEDING THE COMMAND.  THE EDITOR ACCEPTS BOTH UPPER AND LOWER
00087  1    CASE COMMANDS AND VALUES, AND PERFORMS TRANSLATION TO UPPER CASE UNDER THE FOL-
00088  1    LOWING CONDITIONS.  IF THE COMMAND IS TYPED IN UPPER CASE, THEN THE DATA WHICH
00089  1    FOLLOWS IS TRANSLATED TO UPPER CASE.  THUS, IF THE "I" COMMAND IS TYPED IN
00090  1    UPPER CASE, THEN ALL INPUT IS AUTOMATICALLY TRANSLATED (ALTHOUGH ECHOED IN
00091  1    LOWER CASE, AS TYPED).  IF THE "A" COMMAND IS TYPED IN UPPER CASE, THEN ALL
00092  1    INPUT IS TRANSLATED AS READ FROM THE DISK.  GLOBAL TRANSLATION TO UPPER CASE
00093  1    CAN BE CONTROLLED BY THE "U" COMMAND (-U TO NEGATE ITS EFFECT).  IF YOU ARE
00094  1    OPERATING WITH AN UPPER CASE ONLY TERMINAL, THEN OPERATION IS AUTOMATIC.
00095  1    SIMILARLY, IF YOU ARE OPERATING WITH A LOWER CASE TERMINAL, AND TRANSLATION
00096  1    TO UPPER CASE IS NOT SPECIFIED, THEN LOWER CASE CHARACTERS CAN BE ENTERED.
00097  1    
00098  1         A NUMBER OF COMMANDS CAN BE PRECEDED BY A POSITIVE OR
00099  1         NEGATIVE INTEGER BETWEEN 0 AND 65535 (1 IS DEFAULT IF NO VALUE
00100  1         IS SPECIFIED).  THIS VALUE DETERMINES THE NUMBER OF TIMES THE
00101  1         COMMAND IS APPLIED BEFORE RETURNING FOR ANOTHER COMMAND.
00102  1             THE COMMANDS
00103  1                           C D K L T P U  <CR>
00104  1         CAN BE PRECEDED BY AN UNSIGNED, POSITIVE, OR NEGATIVE NUMBER,
00105  1         THE COMMANDS
00106  1                           A F J N W Z
00107  1         CAN BE PRECEDED BY AN UNSIGNED OR POSITIVE NUMBER,
00108  1         THE COMMANDS
00109  1                           E H O Q
00110  1         CANNOT BE PRECEDED BY A NUMBER.  THE COMMANDS
00111  1                           F I J M R S
00112  1         ARE ALL FOLLOWED BY ONE OR MORE STRINGS OF CHARACTERS WHICH CAN
00113  1         BE OPTIONALLY SEPARATED OR TERMINATED BY EITHER <ENDFILE> OR <CR>.
00114  1         THE <ENDFILE> IS GENERALLY USED TO SEPARATE THE SEARCH STRINGS
00115  1         IN THE S AND J COMMANDS, AND IS USED AT THE END OF THE COMMANDS IF
00116  1         ADDITIONAL COMMANDS FOLLOW.  FOR EXAMPLE, THE FOLLOWING COMMAND
00117  1         SEQUENCE SEARCHES FOR  THE STRING 'GAMMA', SUBSTITUTES THE STRING
00118  1         'DELTA', AND THEN TYPES THE FIRST PART OF THE LINE WHERE THE
00119  1         CHANGE OCCURRED, FOLLOWED BY THE REMAINDER OF THE LINE WHICH WAS
00120  1         CHANGED:
00121  1                     SGAMMA<ENDFILE>DELTA<ENDFILE>0TT<CR>
00122  1     
00123  1         THE CONTROL-L CHARACTER IN SEARCH AND SUBSTITUTE STRINGS IS
00124  1         REPLACED ON INPUT BY <CR><LF> CHARACTERS.  THE CONTROL-I KEY
00125  1         IS TAKEN AS A TAB CHARACTER.
00126  1     
00127  1            THE COMMAND R MUST BE FOLLOWED BY A FILE NAME (WITH ASSUMED FILE
00128  1         TYPE OF 'LIB') WITH A TRAILING <CR> OR <ENDFILE>.  THE COMMAND
00129  1         I IS FOLLOWED BY A STRING OF SYMBOLS TO INSERT, TERMINATED BY
00130  1         A <CR> OR <ENDFILE>.  IF SEVERAL LINES OF TEXT ARE TO BE INSERTED,
00131  1         THE I CAN BE DIRECTLY FOLLOWED BY AN <ENDFILE> OR <CR> IN WHICH
00132  1         CASE THE EDITOR ACCEPTS LINES OF INPUT TO THE NEXT <ENDFILE>.
00133  1         THE COMMAND 0T PRINTS THE FIRST PART OF THE CURRENT LINE,
00134  1         AND THE COMMAND 0L MOVES THE REFERENCE TO THE BEGINNING OF THE
00135  1         CURRENT LINE.  THE COMMAND 0P PRINTS THE CURRENT PAGE ONLY, WHILE
00136  1         THE COMMAND 0Z READS THE CONSOLE RATHER THAN WAITING (THIS IS USED
00137  1         AGAIN WITHIN MACROS TO STOP THE DISPLAY - THE MACRO EXPANSION
00138  1         STOPS UNTIL A CHARACTER IS READ.  IF THE CHARACTER IS NOT A BREAK
00139  1         THEN THE MACRO EXPANSION CONTINUES NORMALLY).
00140  1     
00141  1            NOTE THAT A POUND SIGN IS TAKEN AS THE NUMBER 65535, ALL
00142  1         UNSIGNED NUMBERS ARE ASSUMED POSITIVE, AND A SINGLE - IS ASSUMED -1
00143  1     
00144  1         A NUMBER OF COMMANDS CAN BE GROUPED TOGETHER AND EXECUTED
00145  1         REPETITIVELY USING THE MACRO COMMAND WHICH TAKES THE FORM
00146  1     
00147  1                 <NUMBER>MC1C2...CN<DELIMITER>
00148  1     
00149  1         WHERE <NUMBER> IS A NON-NEGATIVE INTEGER N, AND <DELIMITER> IS
00150  1         <ENDFILE> OR <CR>.  THE COMMANDS C1 ... CN  FOLLOWING THE M ARE
00151  1         EXECUTED N TIMES, STARTING AT THE CURRENT POSITION IN THE BUFFER.
00152  1         IF N IS 0, 1, OR OMITTED, THE COMMANDS ARE EXECUTED UNTIL THE END
00153  1         IF THE BUFFER IS ENCOUNTERED.
00154  1     
00155  1         THE FOLLOWING MACRO, FOR EXAMPLE, CHANGES ALL OCCURRENCES OF
00156  1         THE NAME 'GAMMA' TO 'DELTA', AND PRINTS THE LINES WHICH
00157  1         WERE CHANGED:
00158  1     
00159  1                     MFGAMMA<ENDFILE>-5DIDELTA<ENDFILE>0LT<CR>
00160  1     
00161  1        (NOTE: AN <ENDFILE> IS THE CP/M END OF FILE MARK - CONTROL-Z)
00162  1     
00163  1        IF ANY KEY IS DEPRESSED DURING TYPING OR MACRO EXPANSION, THE
00164  1        FUNCTION IS CONSIDERED TERMINATED, AND CONTROL RETURNS TO THE
00165  1        OPERATOR.
00166  1     
00167  1        ERROR CONDITIONS ARE INDICATED BY PRINTING ONE OF THE CHARACTERS:
00168  1     
00169  1         SYMBOL                    ERROR CONDITION
00170  1         ------      ----------------------------------------------------
00171  1         GREATER     FREE MEMORY IS EXHAUSTED - ANY COMMAND CAN BE ISSUED
00172  1                     WHICH DOES NOT INCREASE MEMORY REQUIREMENTS.
00173  1         QUESTION    UNRECOGNIZED COMMAND OR ILLEGAL NUMERIC FIELD
00174  1         POUND       CANNOT APPLY THE COMMAND THE NUMBER OF TIMES SPECFIED
00175  1                     (OCCURS IF SEARCH STRING CANNOT BE FOUND)
00176  1         LETTER O    CANNOT OPEN <FILENAME>.LIB IN R COMMAND
00177  1     
00178  1         THE ERROR CHARACTER IS ALSO ACCOMPANIED BY THE LAST CHARACTER
00179  1         SCANNED WHEN THE ERROR OCCURRED.                      */
00180  1     
